# Git Standard

## Branching

- Branch off the up-to-date default branch. Never work directly on `main`/`master`.
- Naming: `type/short-description` — `feat/oauth-login`, `fix/n1-orders-query`,
  `chore/bump-deps`. Include a ticket when one exists: `feat/PROJ-123-oauth-login`.
- Short-lived branches. Rebase on the default branch frequently to avoid drift.

## Commits (Conventional Commits)

```
type(scope): imperative summary ≤ 72 chars

Optional body: explain WHY this change, what it enables, and any tradeoffs.
Reference issues: Refs #123 / Closes #123.
```

**Types:** `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `docs`, `test`, `style`, `chore`, `build`, `ci`, `revert`.

- **Atomic:** one logical change per commit. It should build and pass tests on its own.
- **Imperative mood:** "add", not "added"/"adds".
- **Body explains why,** not what — the diff already shows what.
- Never mix formatting-only churn with logic changes; separate commits.

## What never gets committed

Secrets, `.env*`, credentials, tokens, private keys, large binaries, build output,
`node_modules`, IDE files, and generated code that belongs in `.gitignore`.

## Pull requests

- Small and focused — a reviewer should hold the whole change in their head.
- Description covers: **what**, **why**, **how** (approach + notable tradeoffs), **testing**
  evidence, and **risk/rollback**.
- Link the issue. Screenshots/recordings for UI. Migration notes for schema changes.
- CI green before requesting review. Self-review the diff first.

## Review etiquette

- Distinguish **blocking** (correctness, security, data loss) from `nit:` (preference).
- Comment on the code, not the person. Suggest, explain, and offer the fix where you can.
- Approve when it's *good enough and safe*, not when it's *how you'd have written it*.

## History

- Prefer rebase for a clean, linear feature history; merge commits at integration points.
- Never force-push a shared branch others are building on.
- `git revert` in shared history; `reset` only on local, unpushed work.

## Agent-specific

- Only commit/push when the user asks. Confirm the branch and message first.
- Use a co-author trailer for AI-assisted commits.
